 ### Gradient Detachment in Training
-A training strategy where the object field component is detached from the backbone to prevent gradient backpropagation to the rendering part, ensuring independent optimization [S25, unofficial]. With detachment, gradients do not backpropagate to the NeRF backbone; without it, gradients from the object field component backpropagate to the backbone, causing mutual influence and a significant drop in rendering quality [S25, unofficial]. The optimal hyper-parameter for empty 3D point losses ($\Delta d$) is found to be 0.05 meters [S25, unofficial].
+A training strategy where the object field component is detached from the backbone to prevent gradient backpropagation to the rendering part, ensuring independent optimization [S25, unofficial]. With detach, the object field component output is detached so gradients do not backpropagate to the NeRF backbone; without detach, gradients from the object field component backpropagate to the NeRF backbone, causing mutual influence [S25, unofficial]. With detach achieves better scene rendering and decomposition quality, while without detach causes a significant drop in rendering quality [S25, unofficial]. The hyper-parameter $\Delta d$ for empty 3D point losses is typically optimized to 0.05 meters [S25, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot K)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (rays per step), and $K$ is the number of samples per ray [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot K)$ for storing intermediate rendering states during the forward pass, plus $O(\Theta)$ for model parameters [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. Chunking reduces peak VRAM usage [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Standard NeRF training on Blender datasets (800x800) takes approximately 100,000 iterations [S11, unofficial]. On LLFF datasets, training may take 250 epochs on 128x128 images [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable end-to-end, produces high-quality novel views [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence due to uniform sampling, high memory usage for large scenes, prone to "floaters" and overfitting in sparse views [S1, S9394-sparse-view-and-initialization-methods.md].
-*   **When to Use:** When high-quality view synthesis from dense multi-view images is required and computational resources are sufficient [S5, S11, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** For real-time applications, large-scale scenes without acceleration structures, or sparse-view scenarios without additional regularization [S1, S11405-acceleration-and-sparse-structures.md].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (rays per step), $N_{samples}$ is the number of samples per ray, and $D$ is the MLP depth/width [S5, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$ for the forward pass gradients, plus memory for pre-computed rays $O(N_{total\_pixels})$ if batching is used [S5, S14, unofficial]. VRAM overflow is a common pitfall, mitigated by chunking rays [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Training on 128x128 images typically takes 250 epochs or 100,000–1,000,000 iterations [S4, S11, unofficial]. For a dataset of 199 down-sampled images, ~8M rays are processed per epoch, requiring ~39,000 iterations for 5 epochs with a batch size of 1024 [S17, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Larger batch sizes (e.g., $2^{14}$) can hinder data loading performance and prevent full GPU occupancy, while smaller batch sizes (e.g., $2^{10}$) cap iterations per second [S15, unofficial]. A batch size of 1024 is a common trade-off [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable end-to-end, produces high-quality novel views.
+*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence (requires many iterations), prone to overfitting and "floaters" without regularization, sensitive to camera pose errors [S1][S17, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-fidelity novel view synthesis is required and computational resources allow for long training times.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time training or inference is required, or when input views are extremely sparse without additional priors.
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial]. It established the standard protocol for optimizing continuous volumetric representations using stochastic gradient descent on photometric loss. The method builds on earlier work in neural implicit representations and differentiable rendering [S24]. It has become the foundational training procedure for numerous NeRF variants and extensions [S1][S2, S4, S5, S6, S7, S9, S10, S11, S13, S14, S15, S17, S18, S19, unofficial][S20, S21][S22, unofficial][S23, S24][S25, unofficial].
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of coordinate-based neural networks for implicit scene representation [S3, unofficial]. The method uses stochastic gradient descent with Adam optimizer and positional encoding to map 3D coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and color [S5, S11, unofficial]. It has become the standard baseline for neural radiance field optimization, with numerous variants extending its core loop to address scalability, generalization, and specific scene properties [S1, S24].
 
